# FR-R3-112 / FR-R3-104 — repository tooling configuration and local observation records. None of
# these belong in a published extension, and two of them are ACTIVELY harmful there: the secretlint
# configuration carries a synthetic GitHub token in its allowlist, which `vsce package`'s own
# scanner correctly refuses to ship, and the two untracked records describe one machine's
# observation of one tree at one moment. Found by the packaging gate, which read the config as a
# credential leak — the right verdict about the wrong file being present.
.secretlintrc.json
.secretlintignore
.gate-attestation.json
.backend-qualification.json
.nvmrc
Cargo.lock
Cargo.toml
package-lock.json
test_output.txt
.eslintrc*
eslint.config.*
.prettierrc*
prettier.config.*
tsconfig.*
esbuild.config.mjs
vitest.config.ts
.specify/**
.claude/**
.agents/**
.schegent/**
scratch/**
scripts/**
fix_ts*.js
out/**
node_modules/**
coverage/**
# Test scratch roots. `.tmp/` is where `tests/global-temp-root.ts` puts each
# run's workspace, and `.vitest-*` is vitest's own. Both are gitignored, which
# is NOT the same as unpackaged: vsce reads this file, not .gitignore.
#
# A normally-exiting run cleans up after itself, so this only matters when one
# does not — a crash, a SIGINT, a killed CI job. Observed 2026-08-23: a vitest
# process took SIGSEGV mid-run, left `.tmp/vitest-50939/` behind, and the next
# `npm run package:smoke` packaged 71 files instead of 51 — audit logs, raw
# session transcripts and a stray .gitignore from the abandoned fixture.
#
# The VSIX allowlist in scripts/check-vsix-smoke.mjs caught it, which is why
# this was a confusing packaging failure and never a shipped artifact. Excluding
# the directory fixes the cause; the allowlist stays as the thing that would
# catch the next cause nobody predicted.
.tmp/**
.vitest-*/**
**/.DS_Store
*.vsix
CHANGELOG.md
AGENTS.md
ARCHITECTURE.md
CLAUDE.md
PRODUCT.md
